WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.840 --> 00:00:03.769
Horror movies are filled with people who make
stupid moves.

00:00:03.769 --> 00:00:08.340
Would-be slasher victims trip over their own
feet as they flee, police never believe anyone

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:13.190
who claims a killer is on the loose and everybody
always assumes the monster is dead just because

00:00:13.190 --> 00:00:13.780
it’s down.

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:18.180
Sometimes, however, a horror movie features
a character so overwhelmingly dumb that you

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.560
almost can’t help but wonder whether they
actually deserve their grisly fate.

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:25.500
Here are a few of the most facepalm-worthy
moments in fright flick history.

00:00:25.960 --> 00:00:27.140
Lambert’s folly

00:00:27.560 --> 00:00:32.400
Ridley Scott's 1979 masterpiece Alien may
have revolutionized the horror and sci-fi

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:36.079
genres, but that doesn't mean its characters
weren’t prone to the occasional moment of

00:00:36.080 --> 00:00:37.600
frustrating stupidity.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:42.640
Let’s put aside the obvious that the Nostromo
crew should never have let Kane out of quarantine

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:46.420
and briefly shine a light on Veronica Cartwright's
character: Joan Lambert.

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:50.800
Cornered by the xenomorph as the crew preps
the Nostromo for self-destruction, Lambert

00:00:50.809 --> 00:00:53.440
reacts by, well, by not reacting at all.

00:00:53.440 --> 00:00:58.289
Chief Engineer Dennis Parker sacrifices himself
in an attempt to save her, throwing himself

00:00:58.289 --> 00:01:02.780
at the towering monster, but Lambert still
just remains planted to the ground, unable

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:05.360
to do anything but cry and scream helplessly.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.760
Unsurprisingly, she doesn’t make it.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:17.300
Parker deserved better.

00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:18.800
Space mistakes

00:01:19.060 --> 00:01:22.450
Prometheus may not have lived up to the high
standards set by some of its predecessors,

00:01:22.450 --> 00:01:27.060
but that’s not to say it wasn’t a sufficiently
creepy movie, even if it featured its fair

00:01:27.060 --> 00:01:28.800
share of boneheaded decisions.

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.319
The first comes courtesy of Rafe Spall’s
character Milburn, who encounters a snake-like

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:35.700
creature inside a mysterious alien temple.

00:01:35.780 --> 00:01:40.280
Milburn, a biologist, decides the best thing
to do in that situation is to reach out and

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:40.880
touch it.

00:01:41.080 --> 00:01:42.960
You can probably guess what happens next.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:44.160
“Get it off!

00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:45.180
Get it off!

00:01:45.180 --> 00:01:46.180
Get it off me!”

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:50.400
Then we’ve got Vickers.

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.840
After the Prometheus self-destructs by ramming
into the spacecraft of the movie’s villainous

00:01:54.840 --> 00:01:59.749
Engineers, the flaming wreckage comes crashing
down towards her and fellow explorer Elizabeth

00:01:59.749 --> 00:02:00.280
Shaw.

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:03.799
The ship hits the planet’s surface and begins
rolling towards them like a wheel.

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:08.369
Vickers, for some reason, attempts to outrun
it, rather than, say, taking a few steps to

00:02:08.369 --> 00:02:09.020
the side.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.220
“No...No...No no no no no!”

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:15.920
Life hacks

00:02:16.460 --> 00:02:21.319
James Wan's Saw helped popularize the "torture
porn" subgenre with a terror-drenched odyssey

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:23.500
of elaborate and grotesque death traps.

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:28.040
The star of the show is Dr. Lawrence Gordon,
the oncologist-turned-prisoner who ultimately

00:02:28.040 --> 00:02:31.060
ends up taking drastic measures to escape
his shackles.

00:02:31.060 --> 00:02:32.840
Except he didn’t really need to.

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.900
Believing his wife and daughter to be in danger
after talking to them on the phone, but unable

00:02:36.900 --> 00:02:40.879
to reach his cell phone because he accidentally
lost it, Lawrence proceeds to use the hacksaw

00:02:40.880 --> 00:02:43.159
to cut through his lower leg and free himself.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.420
Had he thought to just use the saw to pull
the phone towards him, he might have discovered

00:02:47.420 --> 00:02:50.280
his family was safe without having to cut
off his own foot.

00:02:50.720 --> 00:02:51.820
Blowing a good lead

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:56.160
When Scream’s Ghostface killer attempts
to corner Tatum Riley in a garage, she actually

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:58.380
does a pretty good job of defending herself.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.960
Things fall apart, however, when she decides
to attempt an escape by diving through a small

00:03:08.969 --> 00:03:11.260
dog entrance cut into the garage door.

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:12.560
Obviously, it doesn’t work.

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:16.620
And let’s not kid ourselves, either: This
scene was probably nothing more than an excuse

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:20.980
for some close-up shots of a braless Rose
McGowan in a short skirt struggling to fit

00:03:20.980 --> 00:03:22.880
her upper torso through a small door.

00:03:23.460 --> 00:03:24.520
A shocking twist

00:03:25.010 --> 00:03:29.640
Whilst It Follows was lauded for its chilling
intelligence and deft handling of mature themes,

00:03:29.650 --> 00:03:33.870
the movie nonetheless suffered from the inclusion
of a devastating, unintelligent choice made

00:03:33.870 --> 00:03:35.100
by its lead characters.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:38.900
In an effort to bring an end to the deadly
entity that stalks Maika Monroe’s character

00:03:38.900 --> 00:03:43.180
Jay, she and her friends concoct a plan to
trap the creature in a swimming pool filled

00:03:43.189 --> 00:03:44.700
with live electrical devices.

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:49.579
Thanks in part to the fact that said creature
is immortal, this plan doesn’t exactly work.

00:03:49.580 --> 00:03:53.080
Even Director David Robert Mitchell pointed
out the trap wasn’t particularly well thought

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:53.580
out.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:54.080
He said:

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:55.600
"It's a kid-movie plan.

00:03:55.880 --> 00:03:57.940
[...] Something that Scooby-Doo and the gang
might think of.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:01.270
They do their best to accomplish something,
and we witness its failure."

00:04:01.620 --> 00:04:02.780
Laurie’s SNAFU

00:04:03.080 --> 00:04:08.200
In Halloween H20, Jamie Lee Curtis' character
Laurie appears to finally defeat her murderous

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:10.520
brother Michael Myers after cutting off his
head.

00:04:10.769 --> 00:04:14.099
When it's revealed that the man she killed
was actually a paramedic donning her brother's

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:18.410
signature headpiece, however, she’s forced
to face him all over again, and makes a mistake

00:04:18.410 --> 00:04:19.760
that costs her dearly.

00:04:19.970 --> 00:04:23.760
When she’s given another chance to kill
Myers in Halloween: Resurrection, Laurie reaches

00:04:23.770 --> 00:04:27.150
out to pull off his mask and double-check
that it really is her brother standing in

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:27.780
front of her.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:32.260
At the same time, she fails to realize that
his hands are still free, giving him the perfect

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:34.600
opportunity to turn the tables on her.

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:36.940
“I’ll see you in hell.”

